</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h2 id="clear-policy-clinic">A Cancellation Policy Patients See Before They Book</h2>
<p>The same principle from salon marketing applies here, with one addition: because clinic consultations often involve a specific doctor&#8217;s time, it helps to state the policy in terms of respecting that time rather than as a fee for its own sake.</p>
<ul>
<li>State it on the booking page itself, not buried in a separate terms-and-conditions link</li>
<li>Repeat it briefly in the WhatsApp confirmation</li>
<li>Frame it around the practitioner&#8217;s time: &#8220;A small consultation fee reserves Dr. [Name]&#8217;s time for your appointment and is credited toward your treatment&#8221; reads as respectful rather than transactional</li>
</ul>
<p>Applied consistently and stated plainly, this rarely creates friction with genuinely interested patients — it mainly filters out the bookings that were never going to convert regardless.</p>
<h2 id="repeat-no-show-patients">Handling Repeat No-Show Patients</h2>
<p>Most no-shows are genuine one-offs — a patient&#8217;s plans changed, an emergency came up. A small number are repeat cases, and clinics should handle these more deliberately than salons do, given the higher cost of a missed consultation slot.</p>
<p>Track no-shows per patient (most clinic booking software does this automatically), and after a second no-show, move that patient to prepayment-only for future bookings, communicated matter-of-factly rather than as a penalty: &#8220;for future appointments, we ask for prepayment to confirm the booking.&#8221; This protects practitioner time without becoming a confrontation, and — handled correctly — never needs to reference the previous missed appointments directly in writing, which keeps it clean from a confidentiality standpoint as well.</p>
<p>Once this system is running, it feeds directly into patient trust the same way response speed and review handling do — see the full patient-acquisition system in <a href="/clinic-marketing-uae-guide/">marketing for aesthetic clinics in the UAE</a> for how it all fits together.</p>
<div class="gd-faq" id="clinic-no-shows-faq">
<h3>Frequently asked questions</h3>
<h4>Is it compliant to charge a consultation fee at a DHA-licensed clinic?</h4>
<p>A booking-logistics fee that secures an appointment time and is credited toward treatment is an administrative charge, not a medical claim or promotion, and is standard practice at well-run clinics. Always verify current requirements with the Dubai Health Authority (DHA) or your licensing consultant before rolling out a new policy.</p>
<h4>What&#8217;s a reasonable no-show rate for a UAE aesthetic clinic?</h4>
<p>Clinics with a deposit and reminder system in place typically see no-show rates in the low single digits. Clinics with no system at all often run considerably higher without realising it, since the loss is rarely tracked as a specific number.</p>
<h4>Can I mention a patient&#8217;s original appointment when offering their slot to someone on the waitlist?</h4>
<p>No — the waitlist message to a new patient should only reference the new availability itself, never why the slot opened or any detail about the original patient&#8217;s booking.</p>
<h4>Should reminder messages mention treatment pricing?</h4>
<p>Keep reminders focused on appointment logistics — date, time, practitioner, and the consultation fee policy if applicable — rather than treatment pricing or claims, which belong in your compliant marketing materials, not a booking reminder.</p>
<h4>How is this different from what a salon does to reduce no-shows?</h4>
<p>The mechanics (deposits, reminders, waitlists) are the same, but clinic communication needs to stay administrative and confidential rather than casual or promotional — see <a href="/salon-no-shows-cancellations-dubai/">the salon version of this system</a> for comparison.</p>
</div>
